menu "natKit node"

choice NATKIT_NODE_ROLE
    prompt "Node role"
    default NATKIT_ROLE_LEAF
    help
        Which of the three roles in the primary/secondary architecture this
        image is (EPIC TEC-NATKIT-20). One tree, three images.

        The role is a build-time choice rather than a runtime one on purpose:
        the whole point of the split is that a leaf never brings up WiFi, MQTT
        or NTP, so a leaf image should not even contain them. Switching role is
        a rebuild and a reflash.

config NATKIT_ROLE_LEAF
    bool "Leaf (secondary): sensor + ESP-NOW only"
    help
        A sensor node. Reads the IMU and sends frames to the primary over
        ESP-NOW. No WiFi association, no MQTT, no NTP -- it takes its time from
        the primary's timing broadcast.

config NATKIT_ROLE_PRIMARY
    bool "Primary: ESP-NOW hub, timing master, serial uplink"
    help
        The ESP-NOW hub. Holds the registry of which leaves it will listen to,
        reassembles their frames, is the timing master for the 1-second
        broadcast, and forwards everything over serial to the gateway.

config NATKIT_ROLE_GATEWAY
    bool "Gateway: serial in, WiFi/Ethernet + MQTT out"
    help
        The only internet-facing device. Reads the primary's serial stream and
        publishes it on the existing natKit MQTT topic contract
        (natKit/receiving/<Topic>-<id>-Json-<Schema>), so nothing server-side
        changes. Also the NTP client.

endchoice

config NATKIT_ESPNOW_PROBE
    bool "Build the ESP-NOW bench probe instead of the node role"
    default n
    help
        Replaces the node role with the TEC-NATKIT-23 measurement tool: reports
        this chip's ESP-NOW version and real payload limit, sweeps payload sizes,
        and measures send rate with the actual 524-byte IMU frame.

        It is a separate switch rather than a fourth role because it is not part
        of the architecture -- it is the instrument used to decide part of it.

config NATKIT_ESPNOW_PROBE_RECEIVER
    bool "Probe acts as receiver (counts packets and sequence gaps)"
    depends on NATKIT_ESPNOW_PROBE
    default n
    help
        Flash one board as sender (default) and a second with this set to measure
        actual loss. Both must be on the same channel.

config NATKIT_ESPNOW_CHANNEL
    int "ESP-NOW channel for the probe"
    default 1
    range 1 13
    help
        Both ends must match. A channel mismatch presents as every packet sending
        successfully while nothing is received, which reads as total loss rather
        than as a misconfiguration.

        Deliberately NOT "depends on NATKIT_ESPNOW_PROBE", even though only the
        probe reads it. Every source file in main/ is compiled into every image
        (the invariant that stops a role from silently rotting), so a symbol that
        disappears with its switch is a build break for the other three roles --
        which is exactly what it was: espnow_probe.cpp used this as a VALUE, so
        leaf, primary and gateway all failed to compile with "was not declared in
        this scope". The receiver switch above survives its dependency only
        because it is read with #if, where an absent symbol is a legal 0.

config NATKIT_IMU_REPORT_INTERVAL_US
    int "BNO08x report interval (microseconds)"
    default 18000
    range 2500 1000000
    help
        How often the hub is asked to produce each enabled report. 18000 us is
        what ../embeded uses (nominally ~55 Hz per report), and matching it is
        what makes a recording from this fork comparable with one from the
        current firmware -- so treat this as a compatibility setting rather than
        a tuning knob. The BNO08x will not honour arbitrarily small values.

config NATKIT_IMU_SAMPLE_INTERVAL_US
    int "Frame sample interval (microseconds)"
    default 20000
    range 1000 1000000
    help
        How often the leaf snapshots the sensor's latest readings into a frame
        sample. 20000 us = 50 Hz, matching DELAY_BETWEEN_SAMPLES in
        ../embeded/include/BoardConfig.hpp.

        This is NOT the sensor's report rate (that is
        NATKIT_IMU_REPORT_INTERVAL_US, configured on the hub). The four SH2 reports
        arrive asynchronously at their own rates, so a frame sample is a snapshot
        merged across them -- which is what the schema carries and what the current
        firmware sends. Treat it as a compatibility setting.

config NATKIT_IMU_SAMPLES_PER_FRAME
    int "Samples per frame"
    default 10
    range 1 16
    help
        How many samples are batched into one canonical frame. 10 matches
        IMU_SAMPLES_PER_FRAME in ../embeded/include/kafkaTopic.hpp, which makes the
        frame 24 + 50 * 10 = 524 bytes -- the size TEC-NATKIT-23 measured against
        the 1470-byte ESP-NOW ceiling when deciding that one frame is one packet.

        Raising this changes that arithmetic: at 16 samples a frame is 824 bytes,
        still one packet, but the transmit queue item grows with it. Above 28 the
        frame would no longer fit in a single ESP-NOW packet and the decision this
        whole slice rests on would need revisiting.

config NATKIT_IMU_DECLARED_RATE_HZ
    int "Declared sample rate in the frame header (Hz)"
    default 100
    range 1 1000
    help
        The sampleRateHz advertised in each frame header. 100 matches
        IMU_SAMPLE_RATE_HZ in ../embeded/include/kafkaTopic.hpp.

        Informational by design, and deliberately NOT derived from the sample
        interval above: every sample carries its own timestamp, and that is the
        source of truth for a viewer's time axis. It is declared to match the
        current firmware so a recording from either is read the same way.

config NATKIT_ESPNOW_TX_QUEUE_DEPTH
    int "Leaf transmit queue depth (frames)"
    default 8
    range 2 64
    help
        How many packets the leaf will hold while the radio is busy or the primary
        is away. When it fills, the OLDEST packet is dropped: for a sensor stream
        the freshest frame is the valuable one, and the gap is detectable on the far
        side from seqNo.

        The queue is what keeps the sample loop from ever stalling on the radio,
        which is a requirement of TEC-NATKIT-24 rather than a tuning choice -- so
        the interesting number is not how deep it is but that a full queue drops
        instead of blocking. Each slot costs a full packet buffer, so depth trades
        RAM for how long a primary can be away before frames are lost.

config NATKIT_ESPNOW_HEARTBEAT_MS
    int "Leaf heartbeat interval (milliseconds, 0 = off)"
    default 2000
    range 0 60000
    help
        How often the leaf sends its status on the same path as the data: uptime,
        heap, frames built/sent/dropped, sensor report count, hub resets and the
        four calibration accuracies.

        It exists so a silent node is distinguishable from a node with nothing to
        say. It also makes a drop ATTRIBUTABLE: frames the leaf dropped from a full
        queue are its own back-pressure, not the primary's loss, and from the
        receiving side alone the two are indistinguishable.

config NATKIT_TIME_BEACON_MS
    int "Primary timing broadcast interval (milliseconds)"
    default 1000
    range 100 60000
    help
        How often the primary broadcasts its timing beacon (#340 /
        TEC-NATKIT-17). One second is the #319 whiteboard's figure, and it is
        also what the old hub-discovery beacon ran at -- which this REPLACES
        rather than joins, so there is one 1 Hz broadcast from the primary
        instead of two that could drift apart.

        Each beacon is followed immediately by a follow-up packet carrying the
        beacon's actual transmit time, so this interval costs two small packets
        per second, not one. Shortening it fills a leaf's 32-sample fit window
        faster and costs airtime; lengthening it makes the window span more real
        temperature drift, which the linear fit is less able to follow.

config NATKIT_TIME_SYNC_PROBE_MS
    int "Leaf sync report and probe interval (milliseconds)"
    default 1000
    range 100 60000
    help
        How often a leaf tells the primary what its clock fit is, and then probes
        so the primary can check it.

        The probe is what makes the accuracy figure independent: without it the
        only evidence about the fit comes from the fit's own residual, which is
        self-consistency rather than correctness. The primary compares the
        probe's measured arrival against what the leaf's reported fit predicted,
        and the difference is the sync error.

config NATKIT_PRIMARY_WIFI_UPLINK
    bool "Primary associates with WiFi and publishes to MQTT itself (#373)"
    default n
    help
        A deliberately worse architecture, built to find out whether the better
        one is necessary (TEC-NATKIT-30).

        The primary keeps being the ESP-NOW hub and the timing master, and ALSO
        associates with an access point and publishes straight to the broker.
        No serial link, no second board, no gateway. If this works well enough,
        the serial mux (TEC-NATKIT-25) and the gateway (TEC-NATKIT-26) are
        complexity the rig may not need, and TEC-NATKIT-27's adopt-or-discard
        decision changes shape.

        ⚠️ THE CHANNEL IS THE CATCH. ESP-NOW requires every peer on one channel,
        and an associated station does not choose its channel -- the AP does. So
        with this set the primary stops pinning NATKIT_ESPNOW_CHANNEL, adds its
        peers on channel 0 ("follow the interface"), and leaves hop channels
        until they find it. A leaf pinned to the wrong channel is a silent
        failure: every send succeeds locally and nothing is ever received.

        ⚠️ Power save must stay off, which gatewayWifiStart does. An associated
        station defaults to sleeping between the AP's beacons, and ESP-NOW
        packets arriving while it sleeps are simply missed -- so a rig that looks
        like it has a bad radio may only have a dozing one.

config NATKIT_REGISTRY_SEALED
    bool "Seal the primary's node registry at boot"
    default n
    help
        A sealed registry accepts only the nodes already on its roster and
        counts and drops everything else. An open one learns any node that
        turns up and remembers it in NVS.

        Open by default is deliberate: a registry that has to be populated by
        hand before anything works is a registry people disable. Quietly
        accepting a stranger is the worse failure only AFTER you have told the
        rig who its nodes are -- so seal it once a rig is the set of nodes you
        want, and a neighbouring rig on the same channel stops being able to
        appear in your recordings.

        Applied at every boot, so this build option is the state of the rig
        rather than a one-way switch. It is how a fielded primary is frozen
        while there is no downward command channel; that path is
        TEC-NATKIT-26's decision.

config NATKIT_REGISTRY_CLEAR_ON_BOOT
    bool "Forget every remembered node at boot"
    default n
    help
        Bench control. Wipes the persisted roster on startup so a rig can be
        re-learned from scratch without erasing the whole NVS partition.

        Leave this off in anything real: combined with a sealed registry it
        would produce a primary that boots having forgotten every node and
        refuses to learn any, which rejects the entire rig.

config NATKIT_UPLINK_UART_NUM
    int "Primary uplink UART (0 shares the console; 1 is the real link)"
    default 1
    range 0 2
    help
        Which UART carries the primary's framed stream to the gateway
        (TEC-NATKIT-25).

        1 is the design: a dedicated wire, with UART0 left as the human console.
        This matters more than it looks -- #340 measured the primary's console
        blocking its own task for ~87 ms every second on status lines alone, and
        the uplink cannot share that budget.

        0 is a BRING-UP mode for a bench with no USB-to-TTL adapter on it: the
        binary frames are interleaved into the USB console alongside the log
        text, and the reader resynchronises past the text. That works because
        resync-after-garbage is a requirement of the protocol anyway, so this
        mode exercises it hard. The logger is routed through the same lock as
        the frame writer in this mode, or a log line landing mid-frame would
        corrupt most of them.

config NATKIT_UPLINK_BAUD
    int "Primary uplink baud rate"
    default 921600
    range 9600 4000000
    help
        Budget this rather than picking it. Each node costs about
        (18 + 24 + 50 * samples-per-frame + 4) bytes per frame at
        1e6 / (sample interval * samples per frame) frames per second -- roughly
        2.7 KB/s at the defaults. A UART carries baud/10 bytes per second at 8N1.

        So 115200 (~11.5 KB/s) is about 4 nodes and 921600 (~92 KB/s) is about
        33. natVR stalled its pipeline running ~19 KB/s of frames into a 115200
        console, which is the whole reason this help text does arithmetic. The
        primary prints the same sum at boot with the configured values.

config NATKIT_UPLINK_TX_GPIO
    int "Primary uplink UART TX GPIO"
    default 26
    range 0 39
    help
        Ignored when the uplink shares UART0. 26 and 25 are free on the
        ESP32-PICO-V3-02 -- no strapping function, not flash or PSRAM, and clear
        of every BNO08x pin in board_config.hpp.

config NATKIT_UPLINK_RX_GPIO
    int "Primary uplink UART RX GPIO"
    default 25
    range 0 39
    help
        Ignored when the uplink shares UART0. Nothing is received on it yet --
        the downward command path is TEC-NATKIT-26's decision -- but the pin is
        claimed now so the wiring and the enclosure (#320) do not have to change
        later.

config NATKIT_UPLINK_QUEUE_DEPTH
    int "Primary uplink queue depth (frames)"
    default 16
    range 2 64
    help
        How many frames the primary holds while the wire is busy. When it fills,
        the OLDEST is dropped -- the same policy and the same reasoning as the
        leaf's radio queue, since for a sensor stream the freshest frame is the
        valuable one and both sequence numbers in the header make the gap
        detectable on the far side.

        Each slot costs a whole maximum-size frame, so depth trades RAM for how
        long the gateway can stall before frames are lost.

config NATKIT_UPLINK_WRITE_TIMEOUT_MS
    int "How long to wait for a frame to reach the wire (milliseconds)"
    default 100
    range 10 5000
    help
        Bounded on purpose. If the far end has stopped reading and the driver's
        buffer fills, the drain task has to come back and let the queue's drop
        policy run -- waiting forever would turn back-pressure into a silent
        stall, which is exactly the failure natVR hit.

config NATKIT_UPLINK_TX_BUFFER
    int "Uplink UART driver TX buffer (bytes)"
    default 2048
    range 256 16384

config NATKIT_UPLINK_RX_BUFFER
    int "Uplink UART driver RX buffer (bytes)"
    default 512
    range 256 16384

config NATKIT_UPLINK_STATUS_MS
    int "How often the primary sends node and primary status frames (ms)"
    default 1000
    range 100 60000
    help
        The uplink's own telemetry: per-node counters plus each node's clock fit,
        and the primary's health including what it dropped.

        The clock fit is not optional garnish -- the gateway cannot turn a data
        frame's device-relative timestamps into anything publishable without it,
        because the leaf deliberately does not rewrite its own timestamps (#340).

config NATKIT_IMU_LOG_INTERVAL_MS
    int "IMU sample log interval (milliseconds, 0 = off)"
    default 1000
    range 0 60000
    help
        How often the leaf prints the latest reading of each sensor, the report
        rate, the hub reset count and the heap. This is the whole output of the
        sensor slice until frames go over ESP-NOW (TEC-NATKIT-23 / -24), and it
        is what a soak watches.

config NATKIT_STATUS_LOG_INTERVAL_S
    int "Status log interval (seconds, 0 = off)"
    default 10
    range 0 3600
    help
        How often the idle status line (uptime, free heap, minimum free heap)
        is written to the console. It exists so a boot check or an overnight
        soak has something to watch, and so heap drift is visible without
        attaching a debugger -- the old firmware's std::bad_alloc came from the
        frame path allocating per copy, which a flat free-heap line would have
        made obvious.

endmenu
